{
  "passage": "When light falls on a smooth, shiny surface such as a mirror, it bounces back. This is called reflection. The ray of light that falls on the mirror is called the incident ray, and the ray that bounces back is called the reflected ray. Imagine a line drawn at the point where the light hits the mirror, standing straight up from the surface at 90 degrees. This line is called the normal. The angle between the incident ray and the normal is the angle of incidence, and the angle between the reflected ray and the normal is the angle of reflection. The first law of reflection says that these two angles are always equal. So if a ray strikes a mirror at an angle of incidence of 30 degrees, it will be reflected at 30 degrees. The second law says that the incident ray, the normal and the reflected ray all lie in the same plane. A plane mirror forms an image that is upright and of the same size as the object. The image appears as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it. If you stand 2 metres in front of a mirror, your image seems to be 2 metres behind it, that is, 4 metres away from you. The image is also laterally inverted, which means that your left hand appears as the right hand of the image. This is why the word AMBULANCE is written in reverse on the front of ambulances.",
